  "Question": "Two lines intersect, and one of the angles formed is 2 times an angle that forms a linear pair with it. How many degrees is this angle?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "120",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Let the angle forming a linear pair with it measure \\(x^\\circ\\). Then this angle measures \\(2x^\\circ\\), so \\(x+2x=180\\) and \\(x=60\\). Therefore, this angle measures \\(120^\\circ\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Divide 180° in a 2:1 ratio\n\nThe two angles in a linear pair total \\(180^\\circ\\). Since the requested angle is twice its partner, their measures are in the ratio \\(2:1\\). The total therefore contains three equal parts, and the requested angle takes two of them:\n\\[\n180^\\circ\\div(2+1)\\times2=120^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe requested angle measures \\(120^\\circ\\).",
